in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @mirror
in @mirror/docs
in @pack
in @pack/seam
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/property

# @mirror/docs/audit — the audit species of @mirror/docs.
#
# Tick 47 / docs-ouroboros tick 3. Second species of the @mirror/docs
# family-root (#90 candidate, landed tick 45 at commit `f21cc30`).
#
# === The recognition this species declares ===
#
# Adversarial review records are first-class substrate (per
# `@pack/seam` tick 33). They live in their own subdir under docs/
# because their lifecycle differs from spec/insight/research/plan/
# roadmap. The Reed-agent cleanup audit (docs/cleanup-review-
# 2026-06-20.md S-3 finding) surfaced that the substrate had THREE
# names for the same job: `audits/` + `reviews/` + `review/`. Eight
# files split arbitrarily across them; all are Seam-authored
# adversarial-review records.
#
# This species declares the canonical convention:
# - subdir: `docs/audits/` (matching @pack/seam's vocabulary)
# - dating: dated YYYY-MM-DD prefix per insight + audit altitude
# - naming: `audits/YYYY-MM-DD-<author>-<scope>.md` ideal form
# - lifecycle: snapshots; never rewritten in place
#
# === Why this species NOW ===
#
# Three cascading reasons (Reed-agent cleanup S-3):
#
# 1. Naming-redundancy: three subdir names for one job is strict
#    drift; the substrate's content-addressing discipline requires
#    canonical paths.
#
# 2. Pack-vocabulary alignment: `@pack/seam` (tick 33) declared the
#    adversarial-review-frame peer as load-bearing; the docs subdir
#    that catalogs Seam's work must match the substrate-decl name.
#
# 3. Future-cascade-readiness: Seam reviews accumulate as the
#    cascade grows (today added three: Seam-on-@reflection-design,
#    Seam-on-mirror-ref-spec, the round-trip closing C1-C11). Without
#    consolidation, the splitting compounds.
#
# === Tick 3 executes ===
#
# Five files move via git mv (history-preserving) from `reviews/`
# and `review/` to `audits/`:
# - reviews/2026-04-14-fold-operator-review.md
# - reviews/2026-04-14-session-final-review.md
# - reviews/2026-05-30-pre-merge-adversarial-review.md
# - reviews/seam-ast-optics-review.md (undated; preserve as-is
#   per honest-history)
# - review/2026-05-20-seam-adversarial.md
#
# Three files already at canonical path stay put:
# - audits/2026-05-22-seam-mirror-post-meta-glass.md
# - audits/2026-05-25-peer-glass-audit.md
# - audits/option-result-audit.md (undated; preserve as-is per
#   honest-history)
#
# After this tick: `docs/reviews/` and `docs/review/` become empty;
# directories removed implicitly when git tracks no files there.
#
# === Dating discipline (per S-4 cleanup finding) ===
#
# - Ideal: `YYYY-MM-DD-<author>-<scope>.md` (e.g.,
#   `2026-05-22-seam-mirror-post-meta-glass.md`)
# - Backward-compatible: undated files preserved as honest-history
#   (substrate's no-retroactive-rewrite discipline); the convention
#   applies forward, not retroactively
# - Author MUST be one of @pack/peer variants (typically seam, but
#   any peer's adversarial review is admissible)
#
# === The audit_kind variant ===
#
# Per Seam C4 closure pattern: typed substrate variant naming the
# four canonical sub-kinds of adversarial review. Same-shape
# different-meaning forbidden per [[feedback-no-bare-types]].
#
# - pre_loop: review before a /loop discipline starts (e.g.,
#   pre-loop adversarial review of self-improving mirror MCP)
# - retrospective: review after-the-fact of a cascade (e.g.,
#   ticks 12-19 retrospective review)
# - pre_merge: review gating a branch merge to main
# - design: review of a spec/insight before substrate-decl lands
type audit_kind =
  | pre_loop
  | retrospective
  | pre_merge
  | design

# === The author carrier ===
#
# Typed reference for the audit author (peer per @pack/peer variant).
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]]: typed reference grounded in the
# Pack's substrate-decl typing, not bare string.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref.
type author = ref

# === The audit_record carrier ===
#
# Typed record over (path, kind, author, dating). The substrate-
# architectural object @mirror/docs/audit instances are records
# carrying audit metadata.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the quadruple.
type audit_record = {
  path:   ref,
  kind:   audit_kind,
  author: author,
  dating: ref,
}

# === catalog action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING ACTION at @mirror/docs/audit altitude. Given an
# audit file path, returns the typed audit_record carrying its
# sub-kind classification + author + dating metadata.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; the classification
# heuristic reads the filename pattern (YYYY-MM-DD prefix, author
# token, scope name).
catalog(path: ref) -> audit_record { \ }

# === canonical_subdir action ===
#
# Returns the canonical subdir for audits: `docs/audits/`. The
# substrate's convention-over-configuration discipline at the audit
# altitude.
canonical_subdir() -> ref { \ }

# === consolidate action (FIRST non-decorative consumer of
# audit_in_canonical_subdir) ===
#
# Moves an audit_record's file from a redundant subdir (reviews/
# or review/) to the canonical subdir (audits/). The substrate-
# pull-correct equivalent of "consolidate three names into one job."
#
# requires audit_in_canonical_subdir(a): the substrate forecloses
# moves that would put an audit in the wrong subdir. Without this
# requires clause, consolidate would admit misclassified moves
# (the @magic tick-11 dead-letter pattern at the audit altitude).
# The bilateral predicate IS the substrate's consolidation-
# correctness check.
#
# Body discharges via the realisation boundary's git mv (preserving
# content-addressed history).
consolidate(a: audit_record) -> ref
requires audit_in_canonical_subdir(a)
{ \ }

# === audit_in_canonical_subdir predicate (bilateral) ===
#
# Bilateral verdict per recognition #37 (Pask agreement) AND the
# alignment-as-boundary-mathematics check (#57) at the audit
# altitude.
#
# Returns verdict that an audit_record's path is in the canonical
# `docs/audits/` subdir (NOT in `reviews/` or `review/`).
#
# Per Seam B1 closure pattern: consolidate (above) is the FIRST
# non-decorative consumer via `requires audit_in_canonical_subdir(a)`.
#
# Discharge: substrate-pull-correct subdir-classification check at
# the species boundary. Bounded → audit is in canonical subdir
# (admit consolidate); unbounded → audit violates the subdir
# convention (consolidate refused; manual review required).
#
# Same shape as the bilateral predicates the prior families carry:
# - @mirror/docs: doc_well_classified (the parent family-root)
# - @mirror/docs/spec: spec_in_canonical_subdir (sibling species)
# - @pack/seam: review_sound (the peer-altitude predicate this
#   species composes with at substrate-decl altitude)
# - @mirror/docs/audit: audit_in_canonical_subdir (this one)
audit_in_canonical_subdir(a: audit_record) -> verdict { \ }

out @mirror/docs/audit
out audit_kind
out author
out audit_record
out catalog
out canonical_subdir
out consolidate
out audit_in_canonical_subdir
